ouadi Maakoul 

AI:  chatGpt, Grok XAi, Deepseek 

Part I — Mission Requirements

Chapter 1 — Mission Objectives

AMLEV is conceived as a unified engineering platform serving nine mission categories. These are now expressed with quantitative, verifiable requirements that drive all subsequent design decisions.

1.1 Lunar Deployment

NASA’s Artemis campaign targets permanent presence near the lunar south pole by 2030. AMLEV shall support pre‑crew site preparation through autonomous operation for at least 14 Earth days (one lunar day) without resupply. Based on the Lunar Outpost Pegasus LTV baseline, the vehicle must traverse slopes up to 20° sustained (25° transient) while carrying a payload of 800 kg in addition to its own chassis mass. The required uncrewed traverse range per lunar day is $R_{\text{lunar}} \ge 20\,\text{km}$, accounting for regolith excavation stops.

1.2 Martian Deployment

Mars operations must tolerate communication delays up to 40 minutes round‑trip and a thin CO₂ atmosphere. The vehicle will rely on onboard Mars Global Localization (MGL) achieving position accuracy $\sigma_{\text{pos}} \le 0.25\,\text{m}$ (3σ) and must autonomously execute science and construction tasks over 2 km per sol, with the ability to survive a 30‑sol global dust storm with zero solar input.

1.3 Autonomous Construction

Construction tasks include landing pad leveling, berm building, trenching, and road compaction. The target regolith relocation rate is:

\dot{V}_{\text{exc}} \ge 1.0\,\text{m}^3/\text{h}

when excavating lunar highlands simulant (bulk density $1.5\,\text{g/cm}^3$, cohesion $0.5\,\text{kPa}$). The arm‑mounted blade shall grade to within $\pm 5\,\text{cm}$ over a 20 m diameter pad.

1.4 Human Support

The habitable module must provide emergency shelter for two crewmembers for 72 hours. Required habitable volume $V_{\text{hab}} \ge 6\,\text{m}^3$, with full environmental control as detailed in Chapter 16.

1.5 Scientific Exploration

A payload mass allocation of $100\,\text{kg}$ is reserved for instruments (Chapter 19). The robotic arm must core‑drill to a depth of at least 1 m in basalt‑like regolith.

1.6 Infrastructure Deployment

The vehicle shall transport and deploy a 4 kW solar array (VSAT) per trip, and autonomously install communication relay masts.

1.7 Resource Extraction

AMLEV shall feed an ISRU plant with oxygen‑rich regolith (45% O by mass) at a rate of $\dot{m}_{\text{feed}} \ge 10\,\text{kg/h}$. This supports the MARS‑C electrolyzer, which produces O₂ at 1 kg/h.

1.8 Emergency Rescue

The vehicle can act as a mobile safe haven, capable of towing a disabled 1‑tonne rover on level ground.

