Chapter 2 — Environmental Constraints

Quantitative environmental envelopes are established to bound the design.

2.1 Vacuum and Atmosphere

· Lunar surface: pressure $< 10^{-10}\,\text{Pa}$, near‑perfect vacuum. No convective cooling.
· Martian surface: mean pressure $600\,\text{Pa}$, atmosphere 95% CO₂. Convective heat transfer coefficient $h \approx 1\text{--}5\,\text{W/m}^2\text{K}$ under forced flow.

2.2 Thermal Extremes

· Lunar equatorial day: surface temperature $T_{\text{max}} = 400\,\text{K}$; night: $T_{\text{min}} = 90\,\text{K}$ (shadowed craters: $< 50\,\text{K}$).
· Mars diurnal range: 150–290 K, with rapid temperature swings at dusk/dawn.

The vehicle must maintain internal bus temperatures within $-40^\circ\text{C}$ to $+50^\circ\text{C}$ (electronics) and $+18^\circ\text{C}$ to $+27^\circ\text{C}$ (habitable volume).

2.3 Radiation Environment

Galactic cosmic ray (GCR) flux follows the Badhwar‑O’Neill model. Annual dose equivalent in free space is $\sim 0.5\,\text{Sv}$. For a 2 m regolith overburden ($\rho = 1.5\,\text{g/cm}^3$), the attenuation factor is:

\frac{H(x)}{H_0} = e^{-x/\lambda} + \eta(x)

where $\lambda \approx 60\,\text{g/cm}^2$ for GCR primaries, $\eta(x)$ accounts for secondary neutron production. The target dose for electronics is $< 10\,\text{krad (Si)}$ over a 10‑year mission; for occasional crew occupancy, $< 50\,\text{mSv}$ per event.

2.4 Dust and Regolith

Lunar dust is jagged, electrostatically charged, and adheres to surfaces. The Electrodynamic Dust Shield (EDS) must maintain optical transmission $> 95\%$ after 100 days of exposure. Martian dust is finer, and dust storms reduce solar flux to $< 20\%$ of clear‑sky levels.

2.5 Gravity

· Moon: $g_M = 1.625\,\text{m/s}^2$.
· Mars: $g_M = 3.721\,\text{m/s}^2$.

These values drastically affect traction mechanics and excavation dynamics.

2.6 Communications Latency

· Moon: $RTT_{\text{EM}} \approx 2.6\,\text{s}$.
· Mars: $RTT_{\text{EM}} \in [8, 40]\,\text{minutes}$.

This enforces high onboard autonomy (Level 3 minimum).

