Part II — Mechanical Architecture

Chapter 3 — Structural Chassis

3.1 Primary Frame

The backbone is a welded space‑frame made of Al‑Li alloy 2195‑T8. The design is optimized using topology optimization on the design domain $\Omega$:

\begin{aligned}
\min_{\boldsymbol{\rho}} \quad & \int_{\Omega} \rho(\mathbf{x})\,dV \\
\text{s.t.} \quad & \mathbf{K}(\boldsymbol{\rho})\mathbf{U} = \mathbf{F}_{\text{launch}} + \mathbf{F}_{\text{surface}} \\
& \sigma_{\text{von Mises}} \le \sigma_{\text{yield}}/1.4 \\
& \delta_{\text{max}} \le 5\,\text{mm}
\end{aligned}

The resulting structure has a mass of 180 kg and withstands an 8 g axial / 4 g lateral launch load.

3.2 Secondary Frame and Modular Interface

A secondary truss supports mission‑specific modules via a four‑point kinematic mount. The mechanical interface uses ISO 8.8 bolts in shear, with tapered pins for precise alignment. Repeatability must be $\pm 0.1\,\text{mm}$ after multiple autonomous coupling/decoupling cycles.

3.3 Pressure Vessel

The habitable module’s primary pressure shell is a composite overwrapped pressure vessel (COPV), 1.5 m inner diameter, 2.5 m length, designed for 101.3 kPa internal pressure with a factor of safety 2.0 on burst.

3.4 Mounting Rails

Standardized rails (MIL‑STD‑1553 mechanical equivalent) run along the chassis top deck for quick payload integration.

