Chapter 8 — Energy Storage

8.1 Lithium‑Ion Battery Bank

Primary energy storage uses space‑rated Li‑ion cells (18650 format, NCA chemistry) with a specific energy of $250\,\text{Wh/kg}$ at cell level and a pack‑level usable capacity of $40\,\text{kWh}$. The battery model is an equivalent circuit with open‑circuit voltage $V_{\text{oc}}(SoC)$, internal resistance $R_0$, and one RC pair for transient diffusion:

V_t = V_{\text{oc}} - I R_0 - V_1, \quad \dot{V}_1 = -\frac{V_1}{R_1 C_1} + \frac{I}{C_1}

State of charge is estimated via an extended Kalman filter (EKF) that combines coulomb counting with voltage‑model updates:

\text{SoC}_{k+1} = \text{SoC}_k - \frac{\eta_c \Delta t}{C_{\text{nom}}} I_k, \quad \text{measurement } V_{t,k} = h(\text{SoC}_k, V_{1,k}) + v_k

The EKF provides $\text{SoC}$ with an accuracy of $\pm 3\%$.

8.2 Regenerative Fuel Cell (RFC)

For extended night survival, a unitised RFC (URFC) stores energy in hydrogen and oxygen. During daytime, electrolysis produces $\text{H}_2$ and $\text{O}_2$ at an efficiency $\eta_{\text{ely}} = 75\%$; at night the fuel cell recombines them with $\eta_{\text{FC}} = 55\%$, yielding a round‑trip efficiency $\eta_{\text{RT}} \approx 41\%$. The stored energy density at the tank level is $\sim 800\,\text{Wh/kg}$ (compared to 250 Wh/kg for batteries), making the RFC mass‑effective for long‑duration storage. The RFC model is a polarisation curve:

V_{\text{FC}} = E_0 - b \log(i/i_0) - iR_{\text{ohm}}

The system is sized to provide an average of $200\,\text{W}$ for 354 h, requiring $70.8\,\text{kWh}$ electrical output, which at 55% efficiency translates to $128.7\,\text{kWh}$ of stored chemical energy.

8.3 Thermal Battery Enclosure

Batteries are housed in an insulated enclosure with PCM (n‑pentadecane, melting point $10^\circ\text{C}$, latent heat $200\,\text{kJ/kg}$) to maintain temperature within $0^\circ\text{C}$ to $30^\circ\text{C}$. A simple resistance heater switches on when $T_{\text{bat}} < 5^\circ\text{C}$, supplied by RTG power during night.

8.4 Battery Management System (BMS)

The BMS monitors cell voltages, temperatures, and performs passive balancing. A state‑of‑health (SoH) estimate is derived from an online capacity fade model:

C_{\text{fade}} = \alpha N_{\text{cycles}} + \beta e^{-E_a/(k_B T)} \int_0^t |I| dt

where $\alpha, \beta$ are empirical coefficients. Predictive maintenance triggers replacement when SoH drops below 80%.

