Chapter 9 — Electrical Distribution

9.1 Power Management and Distribution (PMAD)

AMLEV employs a dual‑bus architecture: a regulated 120 V DC primary bus for high‑power loads (mobility, manipulators, RFC electrolyser) and a 28 V DC secondary bus for avionics. DC‑DC converters using GaN HEMT switches operate at $500\,\text{kHz}$ with an efficiency $\eta_{\text{conv}} \ge 98\%$. The bus dynamics under fluctuating loads are stabilised by a PI‑controlled supercapacitor bank that supplies peak transients.

9.2 Advanced Energy Management System (AEMS)

The AEMS is a hierarchical controller with a high‑level MPC (Chapter 4) and low‑level droop controllers. The MPC solves the convex program given in Chapter 4 every 10 minutes, dispatching power set‑points to generators and storage. The low‑level control follows a modified droop law for the DC bus:

P_{\text{ref},i} = P_{0,i} - k_i (V_{\text{bus}} - V_{\text{nom}})

where $k_i$ is chosen to share load proportionally to source rating while maintaining bus voltage within $\pm 5\%$ of 120 V.

9.3 Fault Protection and Redundancy

Critical loads are fed via three independent channels from separate battery strings. Solid‑state circuit breakers with I²t protection isolate faults within $10\,\mu\text{s}$. The wiring harness is cross‑strapped such that no single connector failure disables propulsion or life support.

