Part IV — Artificial Intelligence

Chapter 10 — AI Computing Architecture

10.1 Heterogeneous Processing

The computing suite is built around the Intel Starfire space‑grade SoC (July 2026), fabricated on 18A process, delivering up to 75 INT8 TOPS for AI inference at 35 W TDP. It integrates a quad‑core RISC‑V CPU, a neural processing unit (NPU), and an image signal processor. Complementing it are:

· A radiation‑hardened microcontroller (ARM Cortex‑R52) for the safety shield and low‑level motor control.
· A dedicated FPGA (Xilinx Versal ACAP) for sensor fusion and hardware‑accelerated SLAM.
· A neuromorphic processor (e.g., Loihi 2) for spiking‑neural‑network‑based anomaly detection, reducing power consumption for continuous monitoring to <1 W.

The system uses triple modular redundancy (TMR) at the Starfire level for critical command paths, with a voter implemented in the FPGA.

10.2 Fault‑Tolerant Software Architecture

The software stack runs a real‑time operating system (RTEMS) with ARINC‑653 time and space partitioning. AI functions are containerised and can be restarted independently. A watchdog timer resets the processor if the control loop does not complete within a 20 ms deadline.

10.3 Performance Allocation

