Chapter 11 — Autonomous Navigation

11.1 Multi‑Sensor SLAM

AMLEV fuses stereo visual odometry, lidar, and radar in a factor‑graph SLAM framework. The state $\mathbf{X}$ includes the vehicle trajectory $\{\mathbf{x}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{x}_T\}$ and a set of landmarks $\{\mathbf{l}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{l}_M\}$. Measurement likelihoods are:

· Visual: $\mathbf{z}_{\text{cam}} = \pi(\mathbf{K}[\mathbf{R}|\mathbf{t}] \mathbf{l}_j) + \boldsymbol{\eta}$
· Lidar: $\mathbf{z}_{\text{lidar}} = \text{range}, \text{bearing} + \boldsymbol{\eta}$
· Radar: Doppler velocity, range

The factor graph optimisation (Section 6.2) is solved incrementally with iSAM2 at 10 Hz. For global localisation, the vehicle periodically (every 100 m on Moon, 200 m on Mars) matches a panoramic image descriptor to an orbital map using a learned embedding, achieving the required $\sigma_{\text{pos}} \le 0.25\,\text{m}$.

11.2 Terrain Traversability

A traversability map is generated from a digital elevation model (DEM) built by the lidar and stereo. For each cell $(i,j)$, a traversability cost $C_{ij}$ is assigned based on slope, roughness, and step hazard:

C_{ij} = w_1 \tan(\theta_{ij}) + w_2 \sigma_{\text{rough}}(i,j) + w_3 H(\Delta h > h_{\max})

where $\theta_{ij}$ is the local slope, $\sigma_{\text{rough}}$ the standard deviation of elevation residuals, and $H$ a binary step‑obstacle indicator. The cost is then used in the path planner.

11.3 Motion Planning and Control

The high‑level path planner (MILP, Section 9.1) generates a sequence of waypoints. A local planner (NMPC) then tracks them using the dynamic bicycle model:

\dot{x} = v \cos\theta, \quad \dot{y} = v \sin\theta, \quad \dot{\theta} = \frac{v}{L} \tan\delta

with wheelbase $L=2.5\,\text{m}$. The NMPC solves the optimisation described in Section 6.3 with a horizon of $N=20$ steps at $20\,\text{Hz}$, considering static and dynamic obstacles. A safety‑shield (Section 9.5) ensures that the commanded $\delta$ and $v$ never lead to an inevitable collision within the next 2 seconds.

11.4 Mars Global Localization

On Mars, where no GPS exists, the MGL algorithm extracts a skyline from panoramic images and correlates it with a DEM‑derived synthetic skyline from HiRISE (25 cm/px). The match is posed as a maximum‑likelihood registration:

\hat{\mathbf{x}} = \arg\max_{\mathbf{x}} \sum_{k} \log p( \mathbf{z}_k | \mathbf{x}, \text{DEM})

where $\mathbf{z}_k$ are extracted horizon vectors. The algorithm is initialised by dead‑reckoning and solved via particle swarm optimisation, converging in ~2 minutes on the Perseverance‑class compute.

