Chapter 12 — Computer Vision

12.1 Sensor Suite

· Stereo cameras: two 5‑MP monochrome cameras with a baseline of 40 cm, providing dense depth up to 50 m.
· Panoramic cameras: four 8‑MP colour cameras covering 360° for localisation and scientific imagery.
· Thermal cameras: LWIR (8–14 µm) microbolometer for night navigation and thermal anomaly detection.
· Hyperspectral imager: 400–2500 nm push‑broom spectrometer for mineral identification.
· Lidar: 128‑channel scanning lidar, range 200 m, 5 cm accuracy.
· Radar: mm‑wave radar (77 GHz) for dust‑penetrating range and velocity sensing.

12.2 Stereo Depth Pipeline

Depth is recovered via semi‑global matching (SGM). The disparity $d$ for a pixel $(u,v)$ minimises a cost function combining census transform and gradient information:

E(d) = \sum_{\mathbf{p}} \left( C(\mathbf{p}, d_\mathbf{p}) + \sum_{\mathbf{q}\in N(\mathbf{p})} \lambda_1 [|d_\mathbf{p} - d_\mathbf{q}| = 1] + \lambda_2 [|d_\mathbf{p} - d_\mathbf{q}| > 1] \right)

Depth $Z$ is then $Z = f b / d$, with $f$ the focal length and $b$ the baseline. The resulting point cloud is fed to the SLAM pipeline.

12.3 Object Detection and Semantic Scene Understanding

A YOLO‑like convolutional neural network (CNN) quantised to INT8 runs on the NPU, detecting rocks, craters, and other vehicles at 30 fps. A vision‑language model (VLM) provides higher‑level scene reasoning. Given an image $I$ and a natural‑language query $Q$ (e.g., “Is the solar panel clean?”), the VLM outputs a probability $p(\text{clean} | I, Q)$ and a saliency map highlighting affected regions. The VLM is based on a transformer architecture fine‑tuned on synthetic lunar imagery. The cross‑attention mechanism fuses visual tokens with language embeddings:

\text{Attention}(Q,K,V) = \text{softmax}\left( \frac{Q K^T}{\sqrt{d_k}} \right) V

where $Q$ derives from text, $K,V$ from visual patches. Inference uses the Starfire NPU and is triggered by crew or autonomous health checks.

12.4 Thermal and Hyperspectral Analysis

Thermal imagery is used to detect buried rocks (thermal inertia differences) and dust accumulation (emissivity changes). A thermal model solves the heat equation:

\rho c_p \frac{\partial T}{\partial t} = \nabla \cdot (k \nabla T)

The hyperspectral imager applies the matched filter algorithm for mineral detection:

\alpha(\mathbf{x}) = \frac{(\mathbf{s} - \boldsymbol{\mu})^T \boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1} (\mathbf{x} - \boldsymbol{\mu})}{(\mathbf{s} - \boldsymbol{\mu})^T \boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1} (\mathbf{s} - \boldsymbol{\mu})}

where $\mathbf{s}$ is the target spectrum, $\boldsymbol{\mu}, \boldsymbol{\Sigma}$ background statistics. Strong $\alpha$ values indicate the presence of ilmenite, water ice, or other resources of interest.

12.5 Dust and Glare Mitigation

All external lenses are protected by EDS and have hydrophobic/oleophobic coatings. Polarising filters reduce glare from metallic surfaces. In‑software, a dehazing algorithm based on dark‑channel prior is applied to images affected by suspended Martian dust.

Chapter 13 — Machine Intelligence

13.1 Layered Intelligence Architecture

AMLEV’s autonomous behaviour is organised into three cognitive layers:

1. Reflexive Layer — Hard real‑time, safety‑critical functions: obstacle avoidance, motor current limiting, emergency braking. Implemented via classical control and finite‑state machines on the rad‑hard microcontroller.
2. Tactical Layer — On‑board planning, perception, and task execution. Combines learned policies and model‑based optimisation running on the Starfire NPU and CPU.
3. Strategic Layer — High‑level mission reasoning, natural language interaction, and multi‑agent coordination. Uses large language and vision‑language models (LLMs/VLMs) interfaced with symbolic planners.

Communication between layers follows a strict hierarchy: the strategic layer sets goals and constraints, the tactical layer generates feasible plans, and the reflexive layer enforces safety bounds. All upward data flows pass through a SafetyGuard validator (Section 21).

13.2 Large Language Model Interface

Crew and ground operators interact with AMLEV via natural language. The LLM (a fine‑tuned LLaMA‑3 variant, quantised to 4‑bit, running on the NPU) parses intent and translates it into a structured command. For example, the utterance “Inspect the solar array on Hill B and report any dust cover above 10%” is mapped to the logical form:

```
Task(type=Inspect, target=SolarArray_03, location=HillB, 
     condition=DustCover>0.10, action=Report)
```

The parser is trained via instruction‑tuning with a dataset of 50,000 paired (utterance, logical form) examples generated from a grammar. Inference latency is ~500 ms, acceptable for non‑time‑critical commanding. The LLM also generates natural‑language summaries of vehicle state and mission progress, enabling conversational oversight.

13.3 Vision‑Language Model for Scene Understanding

The VLM (Section 12.3) is extended to perform spatial reasoning. Given a panoramic image and a query, it can answer questions like “Is the path to the lander clear of obstacles?” by segmenting the scene and evaluating traversability against the vehicle’s kinematic envelope. The model uses a dual‑encoder architecture:

\mathbf{h}_{\text{image}} = f_{\text{ViT}}(I), \quad \mathbf{h}_{\text{text}} = g_{\text{BERT}}(Q), \quad \text{score} = \mathbf{h}_{\text{image}}^T W \mathbf{h}_{\text{text}}

The output is a probability mask over image regions relevant to the query. Training uses contrastive image‑text pairs collected from lunar analog simulations and rendered synthetic data.

13.4 Reinforcement Learning for Manipulation and Locomotion

Tactile and proprioceptive feedback make direct policy learning attractive for contact‑rich tasks. We employ a model‑based RL approach: an ensemble of probabilistic dynamics models $\hat{f}_\theta(\mathbf{s}_{t+1}|\mathbf{s}_t,\mathbf{a}_t)$ is learned from interaction data, and a policy $\pi_\phi(\mathbf{a}_t|\mathbf{s}_t)$ is optimised using the cross‑entropy method (CEM) within the learned model. The objective is:

J(\phi) = \mathbb{E}_{\pi,\hat{f}} \left[ \sum_{t=0}^H \gamma^t r(\mathbf{s}_t,\mathbf{a}_t) \right]

For a regolith‑loading task, the state includes bucket joint angles, motor currents, and lidar point cloud of the pile; actions are target joint velocities. A reward function encourages scoop fill‑mass (estimated from drive current) and penalises spillage. The policy is trained entirely in simulation with domain‑randomised regolith parameters (cohesion, internal friction, particle size) and transferred to the real vehicle via progressive network adaptation.

13.5 Digital Twin and Predictive Maintenance

A full‑order digital twin runs on Earth, updated with telemetry batches every 10 minutes. An onboard reduced‑order model (a linear parameter‑varying state‑space system) predicts immediate future states for real‑time monitoring. Bayesian filtering (Section 6.4) updates the health parameters $\mathbf{p}$ of critical components:

· Wheel motor bearing wear: Wiener process with drift $\mu(t) = \alpha \times \text{cumulative work}$.
· Battery capacity fade: double‑exponential ageing model.
· Harmonic drive backlash: linear increase per million output cycles.

Remaining useful life (RUL) is estimated as the first passage time:

\text{RUL} = \inf\{ t > 0 : D(t) \ge D_{\text{threshold}} \mid D(0)=d_0 \}

where $D(t)$ is the degradation state. When RUL falls below a mission‑dependent threshold, a maintenance task is injected into the high‑level planner.

13.6 Explainability and Trust

All planner outputs are accompanied by a textual rationale generated by the LLM. For example, “Route A was selected over Route B because it uses 18% less energy and avoids the steep slope at waypoint 7, despite adding 2 minutes.” The safety shield logs every intervention; operators can query “Why was the original command rejected?” and receive a causal trace. Formal verification of critical decision modules (Section 9.5) provides an additional layer of trust.

