14.2 Optical Communication (Laser)

For high‑bandwidth needs, AMLEV integrates an optical head derived from the Artemis II Optical Communications System (O2O). A 10 W fiber‑amplified 1550 nm laser, gimballed with a pointing accuracy of 2 µrad, achieves a downlink of 260 Mbps to a lunar‑orbit relay or directly to Earth (via the Lunar Laser Communications Demonstration architecture). The link equation is:

P_{\text{rec}} = P_{\text{tx}} G_{\text{tx}} G_{\text{rec}} \left( \frac{\lambda}{4\pi d} \right)^2 \eta_{\text{atm}} \eta_{\text{point}}

For a 5 cm transmit aperture and a 1 m ground receiver, at lunar distance, received power is ~ -65 dBm, well above the -75 dBm sensitivity of the superconducting nanowire detector. Optical communication is primary for high‑rate science data return; RF serves as backup and for critical commanding.

14.3 Local Mesh Network

For multi‑vehicle and crew operations, AMLEV forms a 5G NTN (non‑terrestrial network) mesh operating at 2.4 GHz. Each node is a software‑defined 5G gNB‑light, implementing:

· OFDMA with 15 kHz subcarrier spacing.
· Ad‑hoc routing via OLSR (Optimised Link State Routing).
· Latency < 10 ms within 5 km.

The mesh allows seamless handover of video, voice, and telemetry between vehicles and astronaut suits. Positioning within the mesh is augmented by two‑way ranging (TWR) with centimetre accuracy.

14.4 Delay‑ and Disruption‑Tolerant Networking (DTN)

All interplanetary links use the Bundle Protocol (BPv7) with custody transfer. AMLEV’s DTN stack stores bundles when the link is down and forwards them upon contact, ensuring reliable delivery despite long delays. The bundle expiration time is set adaptively based on data priority: telemetry and emergency alerts have 1‑minute expiry; science images have 24‑hour expiry.

