Part VI — Human Support

Chapter 15 — Habitable Module

15.1 Design Philosophy and Requirements

AMLEV’s habitable module serves as a short‑duration emergency shelter and crew transport, not a permanent habitat. Requirements:

· Occupancy: 2 crew, up to 72 hours.
· Free volume: $\ge 6\,\text{m}^3$.
· Internal pressure: $101.3 \pm 2\,\text{kPa}$ (Earth‑normal, 21% O₂, balance N₂).
· Noise: $\le 60\,\text{dBA}$ (continuous), $\le 75\,\text{dBA}$ (peak).

15.2 Pressure Vessel and Airlock

The primary shell is a Type‑III COPV (carbon‑fibre overwrapped aluminium liner) with an inner diameter of 1.8 m and length 2.5 m, yielding $6.4\,\text{m}^3$ internal volume. A cylindrical airlock of 0.8 m diameter, 1.2 m length ($0.6\,\text{m}^3$) allows EVA without depressurising the main cabin. The airlock hatch seals with a silicone O‑ring; leak rate is $< 0.05\%$ volume per hour. The pressure equalisation valve is modelled by orifice flow:

\dot{m} = C_d A \sqrt{2\rho \Delta P}

with $C_d=0.62$, sized for full repressurisation in 30 seconds.

15.3 Interior Layout

The cabin contains two foldable seats that convert to sleep bunks, a compact hygiene module (toilet, wipes), a medical station with basic life‑support equipment, and a crew interface panel (touchscreen + physical switches). Storage lockers hold 72 h of food (2.5 kg/person/day), water (3.5 L/person/day), and contingency oxygen.

15.4 Dust Contamination Control

A dust lock at the airlock entrance uses an electrostatic curtain and a HEPA‑filtered airflow curtain to prevent regolith infiltration. Interior surfaces are seamless and washable. A vacuum cleaner with ULPA filtration captures any introduced particles.

