Chapter 16 — Life Support System

16.1 Atmospheric Control

The Environmental Control and Life Support System (ECLSS) maintains $\text{O}_2$ partial pressure at $21.3 \pm 1\,\text{kPa}$ and $\text{CO}_2$ partial pressure $< 0.4\,\text{kPa}$.

Oxygen supply: Normally drawn from the RFC oxygen tanks. An electrolyser fed by the water recovery system can produce $\text{O}_2$ at a rate of $0.84\,\text{kg/day}$ (2‑person metabolic requirement). The electrolyser stack operates at $80^\circ\text{C}$ with a proton exchange membrane:

2\text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow 2\text{H}_2 + \text{O}_2, \quad V_{\text{cell}} = 1.48\,\text{V} + \frac{RT}{2F}\ln\left( \frac{P_{\text{O}_2}^{0.5}P_{\text{H}_2}}{P_{\text{H}_2\text{O}}} \right) + iR_{\text{ohm}}

CO₂ removal: A solid‑amine sorbent bed (CDRA‑derived) cycles between adsorption and vacuum‑regeneration. The removal rate is $1.0\,\text{kg/day}$, matching metabolic production. The bed dynamic is modelled as a first‑order system:

\frac{dC_{\text{CO}_2}}{dt} = \frac{\dot{m}_{\text{gen}}}{V} - k_{\text{rem}} C_{\text{CO}_2}

where $k_{\text{rem}}$ is the scrubbing rate constant. Steady‑state $\text{CO}_2$ is $C_{\text{ss}} = \dot{m}_{\text{gen}}/(V k_{\text{rem}})$, kept below 0.5% by volume.

16.2 Water Recovery

A closed‑loop water processor recycles urine and humidity condensate. The process train is: particulate filtration → activated carbon → multi‑filtration (reverse osmosis) → catalytic oxidation → UV sterilisation. Recovery efficiency $\eta_{\text{H}_2\text{O}} = 95\%$. The daily water balance is:

\dot{m}_{\text{water, gen}} = \dot{m}_{\text{drink}} + \dot{m}_{\text{food}} + \dot{m}_{\text{metab}} = 2 \times (3.5 + 0.5 + 0.4) = 8.8\,\text{kg/day}

With 95% recovery, makeup water required is only $0.44\,\text{kg/day}$, drawn from the RFC product water or stored reserves.

16.3 Thermal‑Humidity Control

A condensing heat exchanger (CHX) controls humidity to $40\%$–$60\%$ RH and cabin temperature to $22 \pm 3^\circ\text{C}$. The CHX cooling coil temperature $T_{\text{coil}}$ is set such that the condensate rate matches moisture generation:

\dot{m}_{\text{cond}} = \dot{m}_{\text{gen}} = k_m A (\omega_{\text{cabin}} - \omega_{\text{sat}}(T_{\text{coil}}))

where $\omega$ is humidity ratio. A PI controller adjusts the coolant flow.

16.4 Fire Detection and Suppression

Optical smoke detectors (forward‑scattering) and CO sensors trigger an alarm. The fire suppression system floods the cabin with $\text{N}_2$ from the inert gas tanks, reducing $\text{O}_2$ to 15% within 30 seconds — sufficient to extinguish combustion while maintaining crew consciousness for a short evacuation.

