Chapter 17 — Radiation Protection

17.1 Passive Shielding Design

The vehicle’s radiation shielding is a multi‑layer composite optimised for both GCR and SPE. The total mass per unit area of each layer is given in Table 1.

Layer Material Areal density (g/cm²)
Outer structural skin Al 2195 5.0
Water jacket (dual‑use) H₂O 10.0
Polyethylene liner PE 5.0
Total  20.0

The effective dose behind a slab of thickness $x$ for SPE protons (energy ~100 MeV) is approximated by:

D(x) = D_0 \exp(-x / \lambda) + \alpha \cdot x \cdot \exp(-\beta x)

where the first term represents attenuation of primary protons ($\lambda \approx 20\,\text{g/cm}^2$), and the second models secondary neutron build‑up. For a 100 MeV proton spectrum, the 20 g/cm² shield reduces dose by a factor of ~500, keeping the peak dose rate within the storm shelter below $5\,\text{cSv/h}$.

For GCR (high‑Z, high‑energy ions), attenuation is less effective due to projectile fragmentation. The annual dose behind 20 g/cm² is approximately $25\,\text{cSv}$, within NASA’s permissible exposure limit for occasional crew occupancy.

17.2 Storm Shelter

A dedicated compartment within the habitable module provides additional localised shielding, bringing the total to 40 g/cm². In an SPE alert, crew move to the shelter, and the vehicle orients its most massive axis toward the Sun. The shelter has its own independent communication and life support for 24 h.

17.3 Active Monitoring

Three distributed tissue‑equivalent proportional counters (TEPC) measure the ambient dose equivalent in real time. The dose rate $\dot{H}$ (in µSv/h) is sent to the AI monitor. A warning is issued when $\dot{H} > 10\,\mu\text{Sv/h}$ (SPE onset). The predictive SPE model uses a simple flare‑warning correlation: if the soft X‑ray flux (1–8 Å) exceeds $10^{-4}\,\text{W/m}^2$, an SPE is likely within 1–6 h. This triggers an autonomous retreat to a shielded area.

17.4 Electronics Hardening

All electronics are radiation‑hardened by design (rad‑hard Starfire, ECC memory). Total ionising dose tolerance is $> 100\,\text{krad (Si)}$. Single‑event latch‑up (SEL) is prevented by epitaxial CMOS and guard rings. Single‑event upset (SEU) is mitigated by TMR and periodic configuration memory scrubbing.

