Part VII — Thermal Engineering

Chapter 18 — Thermal Control

18.1 Environmental Heat Loads

The thermal control system (TCS) must reject internally generated heat and manage environmental fluxes. The net heat balance at a surface node is:

Q_{\text{net}} = Q_{\text{solar}} + Q_{\text{albedo}} + Q_{\text{IR,planet}} - Q_{\text{radiated}} + Q_{\text{internal}}

For the Moon, $Q_{\text{albedo}}$ is negligible; $Q_{\text{IR,planet}}$ is ~$800\,\text{W/m}^2$ at the sub‑solar point. In the lunar night, $Q_{\text{solar}} = 0$ and the sink is deep space ($\sim 4\,\text{K}$). For Mars, the effective sky temperature is ~$180\,\text{K}$ and convective heat transfer provides additional cooling.

18.2 Radiator Design

The primary heat rejection is via two deployable radiator panels (total area $A_{\text{rad}} = 4\,\text{m}^2$). The radiator surface is coated with Z‑93 white paint ($\alpha_{\text{solar}} = 0.17$, $\epsilon_{\text{IR}} = 0.92$). The radiated power is:

Q_{\text{rad}} = \epsilon \sigma A_{\text{rad}} (T_{\text{rad}}^4 - T_{\text{sink}}^4)

At $T_{\text{rad}} = 300\,\text{K}$ and $T_{\text{sink}} = 4\,\text{K}$, $Q_{\text{rad}} \approx 0.92 \times 5.67\times10^{-8} \times 4 \times (300^4) \approx 1690\,\text{W}$ per panel. Two panels together reject over 3.3 kW, sufficient for the peak daytime load (3.65 kW) with margin.

18.3 Heat Pipes and Loop Heat Pipes

Cesium‑titanium constant‑conductance heat pipes (CCHPs) transfer heat from the central electronics vault to the radiators with an effective thermal conductivity $> 10,000\,\text{W/mK}$. The axial heat transport limit is given by:

Q_{\text{max}} = \dot{m} h_{fg} = \frac{\rho_v \Delta P_{\text{cap}} A_w}{\mu_v L_{\text{eff}}}

where the capillary pressure head $\Delta P_{\text{cap}} = 2\sigma/r_{\text{pore}}$ must exceed the sum of vapour and liquid pressure drops. For a 2 cm diameter heat pipe with grooved wick, $Q_{\text{max}} \approx 500\,\text{W}$ at 300 K.

Loop heat pipes (LHPs), sourced from Eaton (2026 flight heritage), provide thermally‑switched heat transport for freeze‑tolerant radiators. The LHP evaporator is attached to the heat source; the compensation chamber temperature controls the operating temperature via a proportional heater. In standby, a thermostatic valve shuts down the vapour line, preventing heat loss to the cold radiator.

18.4 Phase‑Change Material Thermal Storage

A PCM heat exchanger using lithium nitrate trihydrate (LiNO₃·3H₂O, melting point $30^\circ\text{C}$, latent heat $296\,\text{kJ/kg}$) buffers peak loads. The phase‑change process is modelled by the Stefan problem:

\rho c_p \frac{\partial T}{\partial t} = \nabla \cdot (k \nabla T) + \rho L \frac{\partial f_s}{\partial t}

where $f_s$ is the solid fraction. During the lunar night, the PCM solidifies, releasing 296 kJ per kg to keep the electronics vault above $-20^\circ\text{C}$ for 8 hours without electrical heaters. Required PCM mass for 400 W load over 8 h is 39 kg (Section 5).

18.5 Freeze‑Tolerant Radiators and Survival Heaters

To survive the 14‑day lunar night without trace heaters, the TCS uses:

· VCHPs that passively shut down when the radiator temperature falls below $-50^\circ\text{C}$.
· Louvers (bimetallic actuated) that close to reduce effective emissivity.
· A minimal set of resistive heaters (total 50 W) powered by the RTG, maintaining battery and propulsion actuator temperatures above their minimum survival limits.

The night‑time thermal model simplifies to:

C \frac{dT}{dt} = Q_{\text{heater}} - \epsilon \sigma A_{\text{eff}}(T) T^4

where $A_{\text{eff}}(T)$ drops sharply when louvers close. The equilibrium temperature $T_{\text{eq}}$ is the solution to $Q_{\text{heater}} = \epsilon \sigma A_{\text{eff}}(T_{\text{eq}}) T_{\text{eq}}^4$, designed to be $-30^\circ\text{C}$, safe for all components.

18.6 Martian Atmosphere Consideration

On Mars, the convective heat transfer coefficient $h \approx 2\,\text{W/m}^2\text{K}$ (wind speed 5 m/s) provides an additional heat leak. Radiator panels are fitted with low‑pressure‑drop dust covers. During dust storms, radiator performance degrades; a cleaning cycle (EDS + vibration) is triggered when radiator temperature rises 10°C above the setpoint. The TCS control loop is a cascaded PI structure: inner loop on coolant pump speed, outer loop on radiator bypass valve position, maintaining the avionics cold plate at $40 \pm 2^\circ\text{C}$.

Part VIII — Scientific Payload

Chapter 19 — Scientific Instruments

AMLEV’s scientific payload is designed to conduct geological, geophysical, and environmental characterisation without compromising vehicle primary functions. The instrument suite is allocated a total mass of 100 kg and a peak power budget of 200 W.

19.1 Ground Penetrating Radar (GPR)

A stepped‑frequency continuous‑wave GPR operates over 10 MHz to 2 GHz, mounted on the front chassis. Subsurface interfaces at depth $z$ are detected via two‑way travel time $t = 2z/v$, where $v = c/\sqrt{\varepsilon_r}$ is the wave speed in regolith (typical $\varepsilon_r \approx 3$–$8$). The range resolution for a bandwidth $B$ is $\Delta z = v/(2B)$, yielding $\Delta z \approx 7.5\,\text{cm}$ at $B=1\,\text{GHz}$ and $v=0.15\,\text{m/ns}$. The radar equation links received power to system parameters:

P_r = \frac{P_t G_t G_r \lambda^2 \sigma}{(4\pi)^3 z^4} e^{-4\alpha z}

where $\alpha$ is the attenuation coefficient (tan $\delta$ dependent), and $\sigma$ the target radar cross‑section. The system can detect a 1 m² ice lens at 10 m depth in dry regolith. Data are processed onboard using Stolt migration, and layering is reported to the autonomy planner for excavation avoidance.

19.2 Seismometer

A short‑period (1 Hz) triaxial seismometer is deployed on the surface via the manipulator arm. The instrument measures ground velocity $\dot{u}(t)$, with self‑noise below the lunar quiet‑level model. Moonquake locations are determined by P‑ and S‑wave arrival times, using a three‑component polarisation analysis to estimate back‑azimuth and ray parameter. The distance to a shallow moonquake is given by:

\Delta = v_P \cdot \Delta t_{S-P} \left( \frac{v_P}{v_S} - 1 \right)^{-1}

with $v_P \approx 300\,\text{m/s}$ and $v_P/v_S \approx 1.8$ in the lunar megaregolith. The seismometer also monitors impact events for hazard assessment.

19.3 Magnetometer

A fluxgate magnetometer on a short boom measures the local magnetic field vector $\mathbf{B}$ with a resolution of 0.1 nT. The purpose is twofold: (1) mapping crustal magnetic anomalies to infer subsurface structures (especially on Mars), and (2) monitoring the induced field from solar wind interactions for radiation prediction. The instrument calibration uses a periodic flip sequence to cancel offset drift.

19.4 Spectrometers

Raman spectrometer: A 532 nm laser excites a mineral sample (acquired by the arm); the inelastic scattering spectrum is analysed. The Raman shift $\Delta\nu$ (cm⁻¹) identifies mineral phases by their vibrational modes. On‑board library matching uses cosine similarity:

s(\mathbf{y}, \mathbf{r}) = \frac{\mathbf{y} \cdot \mathbf{r}}{\|\mathbf{y}\| \|\mathbf{r}\|}

where $\mathbf{y}$ is the measured spectrum and $\mathbf{r}$ a reference. A match above 0.9 triggers a positive identification.

Gamma‑ray spectrometer (GRS) : A scintillation detector (CeBr₃) measures natural radioactivity of K, Th, U, and cosmic‑ray‑induced gamma lines, providing bulk elemental composition. The count rate in a peak at energy $E$ is $C = \varepsilon A S \Phi$, where $\varepsilon$ is detector efficiency, $A$ the sensitive area, $S$ the stopping power, and $\Phi$ the photon flux. The system achieves a sensitivity of $\sim 0.1\,\text{wt\%}$ for K within a 1‑hour integration.

19.5 Atmospheric Sensors (Mars Configuration)

On Mars, a meteorological package measures pressure (0–1500 Pa, accuracy $\pm 2\,\text{Pa}$), temperature (150–300 K, $\pm 0.5\,\text{K}$), relative humidity (0–100% with respect to ice), and wind speed/direction via hot‑film anemometry. The wind sensor uses a cylindrical probe with four independent heat‑loss measurements, solving:

P_{\text{loss}} = (A + B \sqrt{U}) \Delta T

for wind speed $U$ and direction. Dust opacity is derived from solar panel current degradation: $\tau = -\ln(I/I_0)$.

19.6 Radiation Detector

The Tissue‑Equivalent Proportional Counter (TEPC) described in Chapter 17 serves double duty as a science instrument, characterising the GCR and SPE energy spectra. Pulse‑height analysis provides linear energy transfer (LET) spectra, which are integrated to obtain dose equivalent using quality factors per ICRP 60.

19.7 Sample Analysis Laboratory

A small internal glovebox with a sample carousel accommodates fines delivered by the arm. Instruments include a miniature X‑ray diffractometer (XRD) and a laser‑induced breakdown spectroscopy (LIBS) unit. LIBS ablates a 300 µm spot and records the plasma emission; elemental lines are identified via peak‑finding and matched to the NIST database. This yields quantitative major‑element oxide composition with an accuracy of $\pm 2\,\text{wt\%}$.

