Part IX — Autonomous Maintenance

Chapter 20 — Self‑Maintenance

AMLEV incorporates a self‑maintenance suite that leverages the robotic arm, onboard diagnostics, and the digital twin to detect, predict, and correct faults without human intervention.

20.1 Structural Health Monitoring (SHM)

A network of piezoelectric transducers bonded to the chassis and suspension components performs active guided‑wave interrogation. A Lamb wave pulse excited at one transducer propagates and is sensed at another; damage (cracks, delamination) scatters the wave. The damage index is the correlation coefficient between the current signal $s(t)$ and a baseline $s_0(t)$:

DI = 1 - \frac{\int s(t) s_0(t) \, dt}{\sqrt{\int s^2(t) dt \int s_0^2(t) dt}}

A DI exceeding 0.3 triggers a high‑resolution scan and, if a crack above 5 mm is confirmed, a repair task is inserted into the planner. The arm can apply a composite patch using an epoxy‑impregnated fabric dispenser.

20.2 Motor and Actuator Diagnostics

Current and vibration signatures of each motor are continuously monitored. Broken rotor bars or bearing faults produce characteristic sidebands at frequencies $f_{\text{side}} = f_{\text{line}} \pm 2s f_{\text{sync}}$. The vibration spectrum is obtained via FFT of accelerometer data; a convolutional neural network (CNN) classifies fault types (healthy, bearing inner race, outer race, gear tooth wear) with an accuracy of 95% on test data. Early detection triggers a maintenance action: the arm can replace a pre‑loaded actuator module (orbital replacement unit, ORU) from the on‑board spares bay.

20.3 Sensor and Cable Diagnostics

Impedance spectroscopy checks cable integrity: a swept‑frequency AC signal is applied to each sensor line; the complex impedance $Z(f)$ is compared to a baseline. A deviation $\Delta Z > 20\%$ indicates a chafed or broken conductor. The system can reroute signals through redundant paths via a crossbar switch.

20.4 Autonomous Inspection and Repair

The 7‑DOF arm carries a high‑resolution inspection camera. Every 100 hours of operation, the vehicle conducts a self‑walkaround: the arm scans the entire chassis, radiators, wheels, and tools, capturing images that are processed by a defect‑detection CNN (trained on annotated lunar‑surface mock‑ups). Detected anomalies (e.g., loose bolt, cable wear, dust accumulation) are classified and a suitable correction action is derived: tightening with a torque‑controlled driver, cleaning with EDS/brush, or flagging for ground analysis.

20.5 Predictive Maintenance Integration

The digital twin’s Bayesian health estimates (Chapter 13) produce a remaining useful life (RUL) for each critical component. The planner (Chapter 22) treats maintenance as a task with a fixed duration and a penalty for failure probability. If the probability of failure before the next maintenance window exceeds 1%, the task is scheduled pre‑emptively. This closes the loop between diagnostics and mission planning.

