Appendix A — Mathematical Proofs and Detailed Derivations

A.1 Pareto Optimality of the Weighted Sum Method

Theorem A.1 (Geoffrion, 1968). Let $\mathcal{X} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ be a convex set and $f_i : \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}$, $i = 1,\ldots,M$ be convex functions. If $\mathbf{x}^*$ is an optimal solution of the scalarised problem

\min_{\mathbf{x} \in \mathcal{X}} \sum_{i=1}^M \lambda_i f_i(\mathbf{x}) \qquad (A.1)

with $\lambda_i > 0$ for all $i$, then $\mathbf{x}^*$ is Pareto optimal for the multi‑objective problem $\min_{\mathbf{x} \in \mathcal{X}} (f_1(\mathbf{x}),\ldots,f_M(\mathbf{x}))$.

Proof. Suppose $\mathbf{x}^*$ is not Pareto optimal. Then there exists $\mathbf{x}' \in \mathcal{X}$ such that $f_i(\mathbf{x}') \le f_i(\mathbf{x}^*)$ for all $i$, and strict inequality for at least one index $j$. Because $\lambda_i > 0$, we have

\sum_{i=1}^M \lambda_i f_i(\mathbf{x}') < \sum_{i=1}^M \lambda_i f_i(\mathbf{x}^*),

contradicting the optimality of $\mathbf{x}^*$ for (A.1). Hence $\mathbf{x}^*$ must be Pareto optimal. ∎

Remark. In AMLEV’s path planning, the costs are linear (energy, time, risk), so the feasible set in objective space is a convex polyhedron, and the entire Pareto front is reachable by varying $\boldsymbol{\lambda} > 0$.

---

A.2 Convexity of the Energy Management QP

The AEMS receding‑horizon problem is

\begin{aligned}
\min_{\mathbf{u}} \quad & \frac{1}{2} \mathbf{u}^T H \mathbf{u} + \mathbf{f}^T \mathbf{u} \\
\text{s.t.} \quad & A_{\text{eq}} \mathbf{u} = \mathbf{b}_{\text{eq}}, \quad A_{\text{ineq}} \mathbf{u} \le \mathbf{b}_{\text{ineq}} \\
& \mathbf{u} = [P_{\text{bat},1},\ldots,P_{\text{shed},N-1}]^T
\end{aligned}

with $H \succeq 0$ because the objective is a sum of convex quadratics (battery resistive loss $\propto P_{\text{bat}}^2$, shed penalty quadratic). The power balance and SoC dynamics are linear, so constraints are affine. Thus the feasible set is a polyhedron and the problem is a convex quadratic program. The KKT conditions are necessary and sufficient. The interior‑point method converges globally at a linear rate and locally superlinearly. For a problem of dimension $n \le 50$ (horizon $N=10$, two controls per step), solution time on the Starfire CPU is $< 5\,\text{ms}$.

---

A.3 Convergence of Real‑Time Iteration NMPC

The NMPC problem is solved by the Real‑Time Iteration (RTI) scheme, which performs a single SQP step per sampling interval. The nonlinear dynamics are discretized by a 4th‑order Runge–Kutta method. Let $\mathbf{w} = [\mathbf{x}_0^T,\mathbf{u}_0^T,\ldots,\mathbf{x}_N^T]^T$ be the primal variables. At each step we solve the QP subproblem

\begin{aligned}
\min_{\Delta \mathbf{w}} \quad & \frac{1}{2} \Delta \mathbf{w}^T \nabla^2_{\mathbf{w}\mathbf{w}} \mathcal{L} \Delta \mathbf{w} + \nabla_{\mathbf{w}} J^T \Delta \mathbf{w} \\
\text{s.t.} \quad & \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{w}) + \nabla \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{w})^T \Delta \mathbf{w} = 0, \quad \mathbf{g}(\mathbf{w}) + \nabla \mathbf{g}(\mathbf{w})^T \Delta \mathbf{w} \le 0
\end{aligned}

Theorem (Diehl et al., 2002, 2009). If the Jacobian of the active constraints has full row rank and the reduced Hessian is positive definite on the null space of the active constraints, then the RTI scheme is locally contractive, and the closed‑loop system under the resulting control law is asymptotically stable for a sufficiently small sampling time.

Proof sketch. The RTI step corresponds to a Newton step on the optimality conditions of the parametric NLP. Under the regularity conditions, the iteration matrix has spectral radius $<1$, ensuring local contraction. Stability follows from a Lyapunov argument using the optimal cost as a Lyapunov function. ∎

In practice, we enforce a maximum of 2 SQP iterations per time step and monitor the KKT residual; if it exceeds a threshold the previous feasible solution is reused (frozen‑step strategy).

---

A.4 Factor Graph Derivation for SLAM

The full SLAM posterior factorizes as

p(\mathbf{X}, \mathbf{L} \mid \mathbf{Z}, \mathbf{U}) \propto \prod_{k=1}^T p(\mathbf{x}_k \mid \mathbf{x}_{k-1},\mathbf{u}_k) \prod_{k,j} p(\mathbf{z}_{k,j} \mid \mathbf{x}_k,\mathbf{l}_j)

Assume Gaussian process and measurement models:

\mathbf{x}_k = f(\mathbf{x}_{k-1},\mathbf{u}_k) + \mathbf{w}_k, \quad \mathbf{w}_k \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\Lambda_k^{-1})


\mathbf{z}_{k,j} = h(\mathbf{x}_k,\mathbf{l}_j) + \mathbf{v}_{k,j}, \quad \mathbf{v}_{k,j} \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\Sigma_{k,j}^{-1})

The negative log posterior is

-\log p(\cdot) = \text{const} + \frac{1}{2} \sum_k \| f(\mathbf{x}_{k-1},\mathbf{u}_k) - \mathbf{x}_k \|_{\Lambda_k}^2 + \frac{1}{2} \sum_{k,j} \| h(\mathbf{x}_k,\mathbf{l}_j) - \mathbf{z}_{k,j} \|_{\Sigma_{k,j}}^2

This is a nonlinear least‑squares problem. Linearising at the current estimate yields a linear system $\mathbf{J}^T \mathbf{W} \mathbf{J} \Delta = \mathbf{J}^T \mathbf{W} \mathbf{e}$, where $\mathbf{J}$ is the measurement Jacobian, $\mathbf{W}$ the block‑diagonal weight matrix, and $\mathbf{e}$ the prediction error. The information matrix $\mathbf{J}^T \mathbf{W} \mathbf{J}$ has an arrowhead pattern due to the temporal chain and landmark measurements, which is exploited by iSAM2 via the Bayes tree to perform incremental updates in $O(1)$ time per new factor, after an initial $O(n)$ batch step.

Proof of arrowhead sparsity: The Jacobian block for odometry factors couples only consecutive poses; for landmark factors couples a pose and a landmark. Thus the matrix is bipartite and its elimination order yields a clique tree corresponding to the square root information matrix. The Bayes tree of iSAM2 is precisely this clique tree, allowing fast re‑linearisation and re‑ordering.

