A.5 First‑Passage Time of Wiener Degradation

Component degradation is modelled as $X(t) = \mu t + \sigma B(t)$. The time $T$ to hit a threshold $D > X(0) = x_0$ has the inverse Gaussian distribution:

f_T(t) = \frac{D - x_0}{\sigma \sqrt{2\pi t^3}} \exp\left( -\frac{((D - x_0) - \mu t)^2}{2\sigma^2 t} \right)

Proof. The density is derived using the reflection principle and the martingale property of the exponential process $Z_t = \exp(\theta X(t) - \theta \mu t - \frac{1}{2}\theta^2 \sigma^2 t)$. Alternatively, it follows from the fact that $T$ is the first passage time of a drifted Brownian motion, which is a stable Lévy distribution with index $1/2$. The cumulative distribution function is

F_T(t) = \Phi\left( \frac{\mu t - (D - x_0)}{\sigma \sqrt{t}} \right) + e^{2\mu(D - x_0)/\sigma^2} \Phi\left( \frac{-\mu t - (D - x_0)}{\sigma \sqrt{t}} \right)

where $\Phi$ is the standard normal CDF. This is used for predictive maintenance scheduling. The remaining useful life (RUL) at time $t_k$ with current state $x_k$ is distributed as $T - t_k$ conditional on $X(t_k)=x_k$, which retains the same functional form with updated parameters.

