A.6 Zonotope Reachability for Safety Shield

A zonotope $Z \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ is defined as

Z = \left\{ c + \sum_{i=1}^p g_i \alpha_i \;\middle|\; \alpha_i \in [-1,1] \right\}

where $c$ is the centre and $g_i$ are generators. The linear transformation $A Z = \{A c + \sum_i (A g_i) \alpha_i\}$ is a zonotope with generators $A g_i$. The Minkowski sum of two zonotopes $Z_1 \oplus Z_2$ is a zonotope with centre $c_1+c_2$ and concatenated generators. For nonlinear systems $\mathbf{x}_{k+1} = f(\mathbf{x}_k,\mathbf{u}_k)$, we over‑approximate the reachable set using a first‑order Taylor expansion plus a Lagrange remainder:

f(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{u}) \in f(\mathbf{x}_0,\mathbf{u}_0) \oplus J_x (\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{x}_0) \oplus J_u (\mathbf{u} - \mathbf{u}_0) \oplus E

where $E$ is a zonotope bounding the linearisation error (computed by interval evaluation of the Hessian). Then the backward reachable set is computed by starting with the unsafe target set (a zonotope) and iteratively applying the inverse map. Because zonotopes are closed under these operations, we obtain an over‑approximation of the true reachable set. The controlled‑invariant set is the complement of the union of backward reachable sets. The shield simply checks if the next state (computed from the candidate control) falls inside the safe zonotope complement. This is a membership test solvable by a linear program.

