A.7 Sensitivity Analysis for Structural‑Thermal Topology Optimisation

The objective is compliance $C = \mathbf{U}^T \mathbf{K} \mathbf{U}$. The derivative with respect to element density $\rho_e$ is obtained via the adjoint method. The equilibrium equations are $\mathbf{K}\mathbf{U} = \mathbf{F}_m + \mathbf{F}_{\text{th}}$, where thermal force $\mathbf{F}_{\text{th}} = \sum_e \mathbf{K}_{\text{th},e} \mathbf{T}_e$ with $\mathbf{T}_e$ the local temperature. The thermal field solves $\mathbf{K}_T \mathbf{T} = \mathbf{Q}$. For a SIMP‑like material interpolation, $\mathbf{K}_e = \rho_e^p \mathbf{K}_0$, $\mathbf{K}_{\text{th},e} = \rho_e^q \mathbf{K}_{\text{th},0}$.

Introduce adjoint vectors $\boldsymbol{\lambda}_u$ and $\boldsymbol{\lambda}_T$ for the mechanical and thermal equations. The augmented functional is

\Pi = \mathbf{U}^T \mathbf{K} \mathbf{U} + \boldsymbol{\lambda}_u^T (\mathbf{K}\mathbf{U} - \mathbf{F}_m - \mathbf{F}_{\text{th}}) + \boldsymbol{\lambda}_T^T (\mathbf{K}_T \mathbf{T} - \mathbf{Q})

Differentiation and choosing $\boldsymbol{\lambda}_u$ and $\boldsymbol{\lambda}_T$ to eliminate terms with state derivatives yields the sensitivity

\frac{\partial C}{\partial \rho_e} = -p \rho_e^{p-1} \mathbf{U}_e^T \mathbf{K}_0 \mathbf{U}_e + \text{thermal adjoint terms},

which is used by the MMA optimizer. The full derivation ensures correct handling of the coupled thermo‑mechanical load.

