A.9 Stability of Formation Control with Consensus

The formation control law for vehicle $i$ is

\mathbf{u}_i = \sum_{j \in \mathcal{N}_i} \left[ \mathbf{K}_p (\mathbf{p}_j - \mathbf{p}_i - \mathbf{d}_{ij}) + \mathbf{K}_v (\mathbf{v}_j - \mathbf{v}_i) \right]

Let $\tilde{\mathbf{p}}_i = \mathbf{p}_i - \mathbf{p}_L - \mathbf{d}_{iL}$ be the position error relative to the desired offset from the virtual leader. The collective error dynamics for $N$ vehicles are

\ddot{\tilde{\mathbf{p}}} = -(\mathbf{L} \otimes \mathbf{K}_p) \tilde{\mathbf{p}} - (\mathbf{L} \otimes \mathbf{K}_v) \dot{\tilde{\mathbf{p}}}

where $\mathbf{L}$ is the Laplacian of the undirected communication graph. If the graph is connected, $\mathbf{L}$ has a single zero eigenvalue with eigenvector $\mathbf{1}$. For the error dynamics, the zero eigenvalue is eliminated by the formation offsets, and the remaining eigenvalues have positive real parts. The system is exponentially stable if $\mathbf{K}_p$ and $\mathbf{K}_v$ are chosen such that for each non‑zero eigenvalue $\lambda_i$ of $\mathbf{L}$, the matrix

\begin{bmatrix}
\mathbf{0} & \mathbf{I} \\
-\lambda_i \mathbf{K}_p & -\lambda_i \mathbf{K}_v
\end{bmatrix}

is Hurwitz. By selecting $\mathbf{K}_v = 2\sqrt{\mathbf{K}_p}$ (critical damping), the system is stable for all $\lambda_i > 0$. This result is standard in multi‑agent consensus literature.

