Appendix E — Extended Concept of Operations (ConOps)

E.1 Operational State Machine

AMLEV’s mission lifecycle is modelled as a hierarchical finite state machine (HSM) with the following top‑level states:

State Description Entry Condition Exit Condition
INIT Post‑landing checkout, solar array deploy, self‑test Touchdown signal All subsystems nominal
SITE_PREP Uncrewed grading, excavation, pad building INIT complete + mission plan loaded Site within tolerance
SCIENCE Autonomous traverse, sample collection, instrument deployment SITE_PREP or STANDBY Task list empty or crew arrival
STANDBY Low‑power monitoring, awaiting commands Any state + no pending tasks New command or contingency
CREW_OPS Habitable module active, crew onboard, EVA support Crew presence + CREW_OPS allowed Crew departure
MAINT Self‑inspection, repair, ORU replacement Scheduled or diagnostic trigger Fault cleared
SAFE Minimal power, freeze‑tolerant, wait for recovery Critical fault, radiation alert, low SoC Ground intervention or auto‑recovery
DECOMM End‑of‑life hibernation, passivation Mission end command N/A

State transitions are guarded by the safety shield. The HSM is encoded in a SCXML (State Chart XML) format and executed by a compliant runtime engine on the safety microcontroller.

E.2 Nominal Lunar Pre‑Crew Timeline

· Day 0 (post‑landing): INIT — 4 hours. Deploy VSAT, establish comm, run BIT, charge batteries.
· Days 1–3: SITE_PREP — Level 20 m diameter pad, excavate trenches, emplace landing beacons. Powered by solar, battery buffer.
· Days 4–10: SCIENCE — Traverse up to 10 km radius, drill cores, deploy seismometer and GPR grid. Return to base each night for thermal management.
· Days 11–14: STANDBY — Park at base, trickle charge, await crew arrival or enter SAFE if no contact.
· Crew arrival (e.g., Day 15): CREW_OPS — Provide shelter, EVA support for 7‑day surface mission.
· Post‑crew: Resume SCIENCE or SITE_PREP for expansion.

E.3 Contingency Sequences

Lunar Night Survival (no fission power): At sunset, AEMS predicts energy shortfall; vehicle enters SAFE. Batteries fully charged, RFC electrolyser off, radiators isolated (louvers closed). RTG provides 100 W to keep computers and one communication receiver alive. PCM maintains battery above 0°C. At sunrise, VSAT redeploy; if power positive, transition to STANDBY.

Dust Storm (Mars): Photodiode detects drop in solar flux > 20%/hour. Vehicle halts, enters SAFE, stows mast. Relies on battery + RTG. Every 24 h, a short‑duration communication window checks for ground contact. After storm clears, EDS cleaning cycle, then resume mission.

Radiation Event (SPE): TEPC detects flux > 10 µSv/h. Immediate broadcast alert. If crew present, autonomously steer to nearest regolith‑shielded area or reorient chassis. Crew moves to storm shelter. Post‑event, dose report transmitted.

