Appendix F — Interface Control Document (ICD) Excerpts

F.1 Mechanical Interface

The chassis‑to‑module interface is a four‑point kinematic mount using hardened steel spherical joints and vees. The interface plane is 2.0 m × 1.5 m. Tolerances:

· Mounting point positions: ±0.1 mm relative to chassis datum.
· Preload: 20 kN per bolt, M12 × 1.75, ISO 898‑1 class 12.9.
· Module CoG envelope: within a cylinder 0.5 m above interface plane, radius 0.3 m.
· Stiffness requirement: first natural frequency of coupled system > 20 Hz to avoid launch vehicle coupling.

Mating is performed autonomously: the chassis uses a 6‑DOF force‑torque sensor on the arm to guide the module onto alignment pins. The insertion force profile is monitored and must not exceed 2 kN.

F.2 Electrical Interface

Parameter Specification
Primary power bus 120 V DC nominal (90–150 V), 2 kW continuous, 5 kW peak (≤ 10 s)
Secondary power bus 28 V DC ± 2%, 200 W
Data bus 1 (deterministic) Time‑Triggered Ethernet (TTE), 100 Mbps, ARINC 664, dual redundant
Data bus 2 (high bandwidth) SpaceWire, 2.5 Gbps, 4 lanes
Low‑speed telemetry CAN‑FD, 2 Mbps
Connector type Glenair Series 79, scoop‑proof, triple‑start threads
Pin assignments Keyed per bus, ESD‑protected
Isolation All buses galvanically isolated (2.5 kV) from chassis

Power handshake: the module presents a 10 kΩ identification resistor to ground on an ID pin; the chassis power controller reads the voltage divider to identify module type and power profile before enabling main relays.

F.3 Fluid Interface

The thermal control interface consists of two quick‑disconnect couplings (Stäubli RME series) for supply and return of 50% propylene‑glycol coolant, rated for −60°C to +120°C. Flow rate: 0.5–2 L/min, pressure drop < 0.5 bar at 1 L/min. The chassis pump is a magnetically coupled centrifugal pump; the module presents a bypass valve to regulate flow to its own cold plates. For habitable modules, additional fluid lines supply breathing air (supply and return) at 101.3 kPa, flow 10 L/min, through HEPA filters. Fluid connectors incorporate burst disks that close upon disconnect to prevent leakage and dust ingress.

F.4 Data Interface and Command Dictionary

All commands between chassis and module adhere to a defined dictionary over UDP/IP on the TTE network. A sample of the ICD command set:

Message ID Direction Description Payload
0x0100 Chassis→Module Module power request uint8 enable, uint16 max_watts
0x0200 Module→Chassis Module status heartbeat uint8 state, uint16 fault_code
0x0300 Chassis→Module Set thermal valve position float32 position (0–1)
0x0400 Module→Chassis Sensor data stream uint16 sensor_id, float32[] values
0x0500 Chassis→Module Emergency shutdown uint8 reason

Messages are CRC‑32 protected and sequence‑numbered. Any invalid message triggers a re‑request. The interface allows the autonomy stack to treat the module as a generic “resource” that exposes a common health and capability interface.

